00:05:51.802 --> 00:05:52.760
And now Cloud Bigtable.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.930
So Cloud Bigtable is the
same Bigtable service

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.780
that Google uses
internally behind,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.160
like I said, pretty
much all its apps,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.000
all its major apps have
Bigtable in their pedigree.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.430
But Cloud Bigtable is the
externalization of that,

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.790
so that it produces a
consumable consumer endpoint

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.610
that our customers can use.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.100
Same SREs behind it, same
higher level of service.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.590
But essentially you can
use and build services

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:23.270
to the scale and sophistication
of, for example, the services

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.811
you see there.

00:06:25.811 --> 00:06:26.810
So why would you use it?

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.500
You need something that's
consistently fast, fully

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.870
managed and infinitely scalable.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.620
So consistently fast,
what does that mean?

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.660
Essentially, single digit
millisecond retrievals

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.970
gets essentially consistent
no matter how big

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:39.270
the data size is.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:43.242
Your P99 is always
around six milliseconds,

00:06:43.242 --> 00:06:44.950
pretty much always
under 10 milliseconds,

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.180
and petabyte scale.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.510
And so the other
implication there

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.910
is that it's also for things
that are sparsely populated.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.000
So if you're collecting
attributes or dimensions

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.267
or measures along certain
keys or a certain element,

00:07:00.267 --> 00:07:01.850
you don't necessarily
have all of them

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:04.700
or they're not all
known ahead of time.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.670
This helps facilitate
that kind of model.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:10.732
These are the use cases that
are becoming really popular.

00:07:10.732 --> 00:07:12.440
Either they're currently
popular in HBase

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.350
or they're now getting popular
on top of Cloud Bigtable.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:18.020
But anything where
you're taking in millions

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.470
upon millions of signals,
perhaps refining those signals

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:25.190
and in terms of updating
them or expanding them

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:27.512
are continuous
measures of things.

00:07:27.512 --> 00:07:29.720
Some of these cases we're
going to talk about slowly.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.800
But keeping track of clicks.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.130
Particular user, click,
what was a property

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:35.250
that they clicked on?

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.220
Or in terms of what was the
internet property they clicked

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:38.510
on, when did they click on it?

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.010
All these are
essentially dimensions

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.560
that keep increasing and
keep having some measure

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.010
that you need to stick with.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.610
So here's the question.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:53.510
So if you have a
user database keeping

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.220
track of things like
somebody's language

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.900
that they want to see on
their website and things

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.500
that are in their shopping cart,
and your entire user database

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.310
fits in about 50 gigs,
that is a great use case

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.980
for a traditional SQL system.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:14.630
That's not a great candidate
for Cloud Bigtable,

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:15.867
or Bigtable in general.

00:08:15.867 --> 00:08:17.450
So it's important
to sort of make sure

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.660
that you're sizing the
write back in technology.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.350
So I kind of liken
it to like I said,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:23.400
if somebody's in
a user experience

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:24.240
and they're not going to change.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.390
Oh, I'm going to go from
like, German to English

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.360
as a user preference.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.680
And you take that for your
entire user base and all

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.580
your user preferences, and
it amounts to 50 gigabytes,

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.544
or pick a number.

00:08:36.544 --> 00:08:38.960
That's a great use case for
Cloud SQL or for Cloud Spanner

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.250
or something like that.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:43.937
That's not probably the
right fit for Cloud Bigtable.

00:08:43.937 --> 00:08:45.770
Cloud Bigtable is
specifically when you want

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.500
to pick precision accuracy.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.660
It's pretty simple to get
started with the service.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.180
It's not too encumbered.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:57.680
This is the UI example, but the
CLI example is equally trivial.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.284
But essentially you
just pick a name.

00:08:59.284 --> 00:09:00.950
So once you pick a
name of your cluster,

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:06.840
or once you create an instance,
you have a thing to address.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.960
And that essentially gives
the connection library

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.940
a thing to connect to,
whatever you've named it.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.684
You pick a zone.

00:09:14.684 --> 00:09:16.100
Cloud Bigtable is
a zonal service,

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:17.902
you pick a zone that it runs in.

00:09:17.902 --> 00:09:19.360
You need to pick
a number of nodes,

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.760
a minimum number
of nodes is three.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.340
And so that's
again why, there is

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:26.810
a certain sort of
minimum size that

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.393
makes sense for data
that you're going

00:09:28.393 --> 00:09:30.320
to put into a Cloud Bigtable.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.450
And then you'd pick
basically SSD or HDD.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:37.060
SSD is more expensive,
but it's more common.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.470
It's a lower latency service.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.840
HTD would be a great
example for things that are,

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.600
you're going to have
a much larger data

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.600
set that sparsed the access.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:47.699
So you can kind of decide.

00:09:47.699 --> 00:09:50.240
And if you really have a true
use case where you kind of have

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.470
both kinds of datas, it
might be efficient to create

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.840
two clusters in that case.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.100
Clients, so I described,
you create a cluster,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.930
clients are going to connect in.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.270
They connect in really
to sort of a load balance

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:05.480
tier, essentially an
endpoint that we give you

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.680
as part of the service.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.880
And that routes to any
number of Bigtable nodes.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:12.982
The nodes themselves, I'm
going to just abstract

00:10:12.982 --> 00:10:13.940
that away for a second.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:15.170
But you connect to a node.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:16.700
The load balancing
there helps you.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.390
And the nodes themselves
then retrieve and read

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.794
and write their
data from Colossus,

00:10:22.794 --> 00:10:23.710
from a storage system.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.209
So it's very important
to understand

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:27.709
that the storage within Bigtable
is separate than the nodes.

00:10:27.709 --> 00:10:29.917
The nodes, you can think of
it as dials of throughput

00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:31.310
and how much
throughput you have.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:34.100
And the storage is always in
a heavily, heavily replicated,

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.890
superdurable file system
that sits underneath.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.319
I call nodes essentially
custodian of keys.

00:10:39.319 --> 00:10:41.360
So then when you want to
access a particular key,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.100
you get routed to the node
that's handling that key,

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.183
and then that key essentially
goes down to storage

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:46.550
and gets it.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.470
And essentially, data
access looks like that.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.660
If you had five keys
here, some nodes

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:53.240
are handling any given
division or segmentation

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.160
of the population of keys.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:11:00.140
And what that does is that
as nodes become hot and begin

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.757
transacting on keys, if
there's hot issues with a key,

00:11:02.757 --> 00:11:04.340
like a key is getting
access too much,

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.923
and it makes more
sense to distribute,

00:11:05.923 --> 00:11:07.759
which nodes are dealing
with which keys,

00:11:07.759 --> 00:11:09.050
the service takes care of that.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.716
It's constantly
learning access patterns

00:11:10.716 --> 00:11:13.760
and rebalancing as it needs to.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.280
And similarly, since data is
not actually stored on nodes,

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:19.244
it's stored in a storage
system, it's stored in Colossus.

00:11:19.244 --> 00:11:21.410
If you wanted to increase
and go from three to four,

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:23.230
that's a second operation.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:24.920
It only takes a few
seconds to do that.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:26.210
Because we're not
really moving any data

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.220
we're simply bringing
up a new node which

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.419
comes online within seconds.

00:11:29.419 --> 00:11:30.960
In other words,
[INAUDIBLE] adjusting

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.599
metadata of which nodes handle
which segmentation of the data.

00:11:35.599 --> 00:11:37.640
It's important to understand,
that in the example

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.580
I just talked about, adding
nodes, that's a completely

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.480
linear upward scale operation.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.910
And so every node can
roughly take 10,000 QPS,

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:48.030
or that's what it's quoted at.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.110
And so it's very linear.

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.484
So if you have
three nodes, which

00:11:50.484 --> 00:11:53.160
is a minimum of cluster size,
you're going to get 30,000 QPS.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:54.580
But that just keeps on going.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:59.550
So if you had essentially
30, that's 3 in 1,000.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:03.432
And 300, that's 3 million.

00:12:03.432 --> 00:12:06.492
Hopefully I get the math
right because it's on YouTube.

00:12:06.492 --> 00:12:07.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:08.820
But it is.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.010
We had a great talk last
year we were hitting,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.210
I think 25 million QPS.

00:12:14.596 --> 00:12:15.720
It was in last year's Next.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.820
So these are real numbers,
and essentially we

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.687
have real customers doing
these things at scale.

00:12:20.687 --> 00:12:23.020
Again, with the spirit of
just how do you connect to it.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:24.420
So we have a cluster,
we have a name,

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.130
we have a name of the thing
that we can connect to.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.713
And it has sides, and you know
what you're reading and writing

00:12:28.713 --> 00:12:29.370
there.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:30.745
You can connect
with an existing,

00:12:30.745 --> 00:12:32.880
so if you have a an
HBase client, essentially

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.430
a Java client, you can
continue to use that.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.740
We put out a Go client,
a Python client.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:40.740
And there are some community
clients for things like Skala

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:42.771
and .NET.

00:12:42.771 --> 00:12:45.270
But these are the clients that
Google specifically puts out.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:51.100
Once you have a client,
once you have a cluster

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.700
and you have say, for
example, an HBas client or we

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.530
provide a CLI, you can get in.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.290
And this is sort of like being
in your administrative session

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.390
inside of the databases
where you can make

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.190
tables inside your clusters.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.990
And then that CF stands
for column family,

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:05.531
and I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:13:05.531 --> 00:13:07.630
But you have a cluster,
you can connect to it,

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:10.860
and then you can start to
take on admin operations,

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:13.140
or admin plane
operations inside of it.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.415
That's all stuff you can
kind of take a look at.

00:13:16.415 --> 00:13:18.540
So if you have an existing
HBase deployment, things

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.430
you're already doing
in code in HBase.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.860
And you say, hey, I want to
take this HBase application,

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.545
and how do I use it with
Bigtable, it's really simple.

00:13:25.545 --> 00:13:27.990
You simply change
the connection string

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:31.200
from being the HBase
canonical connection factory

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.060
to a Bigtable connect object.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.670
And then an
additional import that

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.170
just says that you're going to
use a Bigtable configuration.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.600
That's approach one,
that's the simplest.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.100
There's another
approach which says,

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.060
I don't want to touch my code.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:45.851
My code is running.

00:13:45.851 --> 00:13:48.090
Or maybe I want to leave
my code as agnostic,

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.400
which just means outside
of your application code.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.970
You can essentially introduce
a small HBase site, XML,

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:56.930
which basically populates your
instance, your project ID.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:59.520
Hopefully everybody knows
what a project ID is now.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:02.300
And then you can choose
to include that in your

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:03.000
build file.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.220
So bottom line,
it's very, very easy

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.550
to go make the switch from
HBase to Cloud Bigtable.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.250
It's pretty trivial.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:14.700
Moving up, we're moving up
from inception to what it is,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:19.205
to how to use it, to what does
it look like internally here.

00:14:19.205 --> 00:14:19.830
The data model.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.474
So data model, it's
NoSQL, no-join,

00:14:22.474 --> 00:14:23.640
distributed key-value store.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.056
So essentially you
have mechanisms

00:14:25.056 --> 00:14:28.744
to retrieve keys
very, very fast.

00:14:28.744 --> 00:14:31.160
We'll talk about some other
operations you can do on keys.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:32.160
But it's only one index.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.040
It's like having a table with
one index, which is essentially

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.560
the row key, as it's called.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.934
And you can do atomic
operations inside

00:14:40.934 --> 00:14:43.350
of a particular row key, which
means that basically if you

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.060
decide to update
something or decrement

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.450
and increase something in the
context of a particular row

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:49.620
key, then that works.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:51.930
But you can't do anything
like for example,

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:55.800
join information that's in
one key with another key.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.900
And nor can you say, let me for
example, decrement from key A

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:06.021
and plenish it into Key B.
That would be essentially

00:15:06.021 --> 00:15:07.840
a cross-key transaction.

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:09.540
So again, you can
do things with a key

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.710
and know that that's
consistent within that key,

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.950
but not across keys.

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:15.060
And then it's sparse.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:19.290
So if you haven't written
a particular column,

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:20.790
then it's not taking up space.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:22.248
You don't have to
worry about that.

00:15:22.248 --> 00:15:23.470
In fact, that's encouraged.

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.428
If you haven't taken a
particular reading type,

00:15:25.428 --> 00:15:27.990
or you don't have a particular
dimension on what you're

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:31.794
reading, then you don't have to
worry about that being wasted

00:15:31.794 --> 00:15:33.210
space, much like
you would perhaps

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.420
see that as being wasted space
in a traditional RDBMS system.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:43.700
So when you have a row key,
there's a couple of things.

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:46.986
Actually, let me go back.

00:15:46.986 --> 00:15:48.110
I skipped a section, sorry.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:51.200
So after the row key, you have
this notion of column families

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:54.189
that was in that previous,
when I showed the HBase shell.

00:15:54.189 --> 00:15:56.480
So the column family is sort
of things that are roughly

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.610
grouped together,
which is just sort

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:03.080
of like a macro qualifier
on individual smaller

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.240
elements, which are
called common qualifiers.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:10.910
And so if you wanted to refer
to any given cell in this table,

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:14.120
you would specify the row key,
and then which column family

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.400
and which qualifier
you are interested in.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:19.400
And essentially that zeros
you in on the exact element

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:22.155
that you want to retrieve.

00:16:22.155 --> 00:16:24.530
The cells themselves, these
individual cells, [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:25.530
you zeroed in on a cell.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:28.310
The cells themselves-- say
that fast everybody, together.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:31.820
So the cells themselves
are versioned.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.910
So what that means
is as you write data,

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:37.560
the data has this sort
of implied version,

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:41.720
which is N64, which
is a flavor timestamp.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.150
You can technically make
it anything you want.

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.430
But the default is, it's
the current timestamp.

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:50.300
And so if you were to write
the same cell again, or do

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:54.650
the same put again, or do a put
in the same qualifier again,

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:59.570
it would simply layer on at the
latest timestamp the new value.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.520
And the old ones
wouldn't be there.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.040
And by default, the old
ones will get garbage

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:05.041
collected at some point.

00:17:05.041 --> 00:17:07.540
And so that's why you can have
a very high throughput that's

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:08.300
very consistent.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:11.869
You can choose to have
a retention policy that

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:15.109
doesn't expire, or keeps a
number of copies or something

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:16.020
like that.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.270
But the default is that
you just keep layering up.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:22.369
And at some point the old
versions get wiped away.

00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:26.869
So what are the operations?

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:30.040
So in terms of getting
data in, you can do put.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:30.965
It's a canonical put.

00:17:30.965 --> 00:17:32.960
You can put data in,
which is putting one.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.360
You can increment, so
whatever the value is,

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:38.070
then that happens on the server
side where it can increment.

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:41.240
Append, or if you just want to
canonically append something,

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:42.710
you can do conditional updates.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:45.560
So if the value is x, then
only if the value is x,

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.000
I want to do y.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.940
And then bulk import
by using some sort

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:53.000
of map-reduced
process or data flow.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.536
Getting data out, you can get.

00:17:54.536 --> 00:17:55.910
So basically it's
just like, this

00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:58.220
is the row key, get me that.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.010
Range scan, that
says I'm basically

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:05.000
going to start this pattern
and at this pattern.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.541
Range of the scan
and this end, we're

00:18:07.541 --> 00:18:09.290
going to hear some
very interesting things

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.132
you can do with a range can
towards the end of the talk.

00:18:12.132 --> 00:18:14.090
Filter, which basically
means after I get this,

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:16.017
I want to filter what I'm using.

00:18:16.017 --> 00:18:18.350
Full scan, literally full
scan is always the worst thing

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:19.430
you can do in a database.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.220
But it's from top to
bottom, essentially

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:22.178
get me the whole thing.

00:18:22.178 --> 00:18:24.740
And then an export, again
using some bulk tools

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:29.454
like MapReduce job or data flow.

00:18:29.454 --> 00:18:30.620
All right, so schema design.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.950
Basics, so you have
rows, you have row keys.

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:40.351
So you would store some
of the big things here--

00:18:40.351 --> 00:18:42.600
I'll let you kind of glance
through this, some of this

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.990
has already been
talked about before.

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:48.180
But highlighting things like
you want to avoid on your keys,

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:51.330
you want to avoid things that
are monotomically increasing.

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.572
You want to avoid choosing,
for example, a timestamp

00:18:53.572 --> 00:18:55.530
or a sequence number or
something that's always

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.780
increasing, because
that will potentially

00:18:57.780 --> 00:18:59.724
create some hotspots
in your architecture.

00:18:59.724 --> 00:19:01.890
There's some strategies
we'll talk about in a second

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.140
to help avoid that.

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:07.404
The other is, if you're going
to have something that's--

00:19:07.404 --> 00:19:09.570
store things that are related
to a particular entity

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:10.440
in the same row.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:13.440
If you can't do that
because of some size

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:17.150
or for some other
restrictions, then you

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:18.900
can essentially think
about splitting them

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:23.370
up and using your key as a way
to group like items together.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:26.310
Store related entities together.

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.840
We're going to talk about that
in a couple slides as well.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.804
But just keep in
mind, essentially

00:19:30.804 --> 00:19:32.220
when you don't
have a lot of knobs

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:36.304
to turn in a NoSQL
system, what can you turn?

00:19:36.304 --> 00:19:38.220
Those things that you
can turn are essentially

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:42.540
the row key and how
you pick your row key,

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:45.360
and then what you're going
to keep in your rows,

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:48.385
and how that information
can be stored and retrieved.

00:19:51.311 --> 00:19:52.560
And so what do I mean by that?

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.143
So you have to start
getting creative.

00:19:54.143 --> 00:19:57.700
So if I just say, memory
uses as a row key,

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.360
it's going to need
essentially an element that's

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.330
always receiving an update.

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:05.730
And so it's going to
create a certain hotness

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:09.390
for that particular row key,
because it's pretty simplistic.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:12.360
So we do something here we
call field promotion, which

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.780
is we take something that
was originally a column,

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.680
and we shove it over to
be essentially combined

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:20.970
with a row key.

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:23.145
So in this case, if
I take timestamp out

00:20:23.145 --> 00:20:27.150
of being simply column
data, and move it

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.880
and combine it with what was
the original name, which is

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:31.560
memory usage, that helps a lot.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:35.520
Because now it's sort
of added some variation

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:39.667
to something that was a
static element before.

00:20:39.667 --> 00:20:42.000
But the problem is, even that
timestamp then within that

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.800
may start to get hot.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.050
And so you could think of
hey, if I take another field

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:48.667
and promote that,
which is user, and put

00:20:48.667 --> 00:20:50.250
that in, that
essentially is basically

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:54.160
creating shards of the way
you can query your data.

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:58.350
It doesn't effectively
create shards,

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.180
but it basically helps
distribute that data

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:02.992
into discrete different
locations within your system.

00:21:02.992 --> 00:21:04.200
There's other techniques too.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.810
This is called field promotion,
this is the most popular one.

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.270
We'll talk about that some more.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.670
You could use randomization,
you could use hashing,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:13.560
there's other things you can do.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.990
But if, for example, if you
choose to hash something,

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:17.940
then just remember that
that's always going

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.350
to be a client-side operation.

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.054
You could choose
to salt your keys.

00:21:21.054 --> 00:21:22.470
There's different
techniques here,

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.011
this is the most
prevalent one, which

00:21:24.011 --> 00:21:26.754
is to simply field promote.

00:21:26.754 --> 00:21:28.170
And field promotion
is interesting

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:34.500
because then what will
you do, is it sort of

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:38.860
gives rise to just how
creative you can be with that.

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:41.880
So there's a design element here
called tall versus wide tables.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.750
And so in a wide
table, essentially you

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:46.872
have an element.

00:21:46.872 --> 00:21:48.330
This is a Prezi
example that we use

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:51.090
on our social network
involving presidents, which

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:54.880
is kind of ironic nowadays.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:58.440
But if you had essentially
hey, for this given key,

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:00.540
tell me everything
about it, that's

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:03.960
an example of a wide table.

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:07.110
The width of your table should
generally have a horizon.

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.061
If it has no horizon,
like keeping track,

00:22:09.061 --> 00:22:10.560
like hey, I'm going
to make a column

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.143
for every single
timestamp and it just

00:22:12.143 --> 00:22:14.670
goes on and on and on,
literally into infinity,

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.290
that's not a good design choice.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:20.610
Because essentially row sizes
should be kept in general,

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.500
any given row size
should be kept in general

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:26.080
under 100 megabytes.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:28.530
So if you think that's the
case, then you probably

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.330
want to change your
schema a little bit,

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:34.380
maybe break that up instead
of it being off into infinity.

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:37.350
Maybe you say well, my row
key is some element and then

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:40.480
the day, so at least
that row has some horizon

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:41.730
that it's going to finish out.

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.188
But having a row design that
makes it so that you're always

00:22:44.188 --> 00:22:49.230
forever going to have ever
expanding columns is a problem.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.097
So that's wide.

00:22:50.097 --> 00:22:52.680
If you've got a query pattern,
I'm going to ask this question,

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:54.570
I want to know
everything about it.

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.528
There's a different kind
of question, which is,

00:22:56.528 --> 00:22:57.854
does this relationship exist?

00:22:57.854 --> 00:22:59.520
And the way you could
kind of mimic this

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.410
is to say, well, let me
concatenate-- for example,

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:01.951
two elements.

00:23:01.951 --> 00:23:03.990
Let me see if they're related.

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:08.277
Like in this example, where
the names have been hashed

00:23:08.277 --> 00:23:09.360
and concatenated together.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.250
And so if you were
looking for that,

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:13.265
you could just say like,
OK, get me this key.

00:23:13.265 --> 00:23:14.640
The absence of
that key tells you

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.510
that the relationship
doesn't exist.

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:19.291
And if you in fact,
get that key, then

00:23:19.291 --> 00:23:21.540
the elements you'd retrieve
would give you essentially

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.584
the dictionary of all the
items that you would see,

00:23:24.584 --> 00:23:27.000
that would tell you that not
only the relationship exists,

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:28.916
but everything coming
back is what's relevant.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.850
After everything I just told
you, I will say, don't do that.

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:39.049
So if you want to get that
low level in terms of the data

00:23:39.049 --> 00:23:41.090
model and how to use it
and how to incorporate it

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.550
in your applications,
by all means do so.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.800
And there are a number of
projects that come out.

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.810
The best place to start
is from the top down.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:49.874
If you're going to start
using Cloud Bigtable,

00:23:49.874 --> 00:23:51.290
the best place to
start is to say,

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:55.130
is there a project framework
piece of open source software

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:59.180
that I can leverage to help
solve this problem that's

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:03.245
already figured out how to do
this effectively with Bigtable,

00:24:03.245 --> 00:24:04.510
or a NoSQL database?

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:05.660
This is an example of them.

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:09.437
A graph database is
janusGraph, OpenTSDB we're

00:24:09.437 --> 00:24:10.270
about to hear about.

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:15.427
And there's other time
series monitoring databases.

00:24:15.427 --> 00:24:17.510
GeoMesa we're going to
hear about in a second too.

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:19.100
And then on top of
that, Google itself

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.900
has deep integrations
with some of our products.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:28.340
So with that, I would like to
go into one of those use cases

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:30.560
and ask David Mytton, this
is the co-founder and CEO

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.435
of Server Density, to
talk about time series.

00:24:32.435 --> 00:24:33.510
DAVID MYTTON: Thank you.

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:40.840
Afternoon, everybody.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:42.300
So my name's David Mytton.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:45.830
I'm the co-founder and
CEO at Server Density.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:50.320
And we do systems availability
and performance monitoring.

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:54.210
So we have an agent that
exists on your servers,

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:56.460
sends data into our
system, and we also

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.370
do availability monitoring from
locations around the world,

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:02.340
so you get that internal
and external view.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:05.400
And everything you can
see on this dashboard

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:10.100
is essentially now powered
by Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:13.500
So the graphs pulling
out the latest values,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.630
generating calculations
on uptime, this

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:20.479
is all powered by our new
integration with Bigtable.

00:25:20.479 --> 00:25:22.020
So what I'm going
to talk you through

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:25.470
is a quick run-through
of the old system.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:27.180
We've been in business
for seven years.

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:30.720
And the old system
lasted about six years.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:33.660
The challenges that
we faced last year,

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:38.670
and why we decided to
pick OpenTSDB and Bigtable

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.022
as the time series
storage engine.

00:25:41.022 --> 00:25:43.230
We're going to talk a little
bit about the migration,

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:46.790
which we did without
any customer downtime,

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:51.114
how the new system looks, and
then two lessons that we learnt

00:25:51.114 --> 00:25:52.530
whilst we were
doing the migration

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:55.690
and the implementation.

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:58.770
So the first language
that I learnt was PHP.

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:04.170
And back in 2009, there were
no time series databases

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.790
that would work for us.

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.373
And there was nothing
available, there

00:26:07.373 --> 00:26:08.880
were no products,
nor no services.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:14.430
And MongoDB was very new at
the time, it was pre version 1.

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:16.680
And it worked
really well at doing

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:18.960
really fast writes,
had some pretty

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.270
good drivers for the languages.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:26.340
And decided to use it as the
time series data format storage

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:27.506
system.

00:26:27.506 --> 00:26:29.130
So the way that this
works is that data

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:31.170
comes into us on a push basis.

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:33.370
You install the agent
into your system.

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:37.260
It sends data to us, it's just
the JSON payload over HTTPS.

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:42.129
It comes into our system, and
then it goes into the queue.

00:26:42.129 --> 00:26:43.920
We played around with
RabbitMQ and couldn't

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:45.720
get it to scale over
multiple data centers,

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.760
so we implemented our
own basic queuing system

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:49.680
on top of MongoDB.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.270
That then passes it through
into a rules engine.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:54.060
So when you configure
your alerts,

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:56.684
it looks at whether a
threshold has been met,

00:26:56.684 --> 00:26:58.350
and whether we need
to trigger an alert,

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:01.710
or maybe close an
already open alert.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.590
It then sends it off to
a notification system.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:06.510
And I started
[INAUDIBLE] as a project

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:09.210
to learn Python, which is what
our monitoring agent is written

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:09.780
in.

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:12.930
And the notification system
was written in Python,

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:15.780
so that's sending
emails and SMSes.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.850
And then the second
route is to send off

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:21.840
into the time series data
storage, which was MongoDB.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.850
So when you load a
dashboard, it would

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:27.990
query an internal API, which
would then query Mongo and pull

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:30.502
the data out.

00:27:30.502 --> 00:27:34.830
Well, this system's scaled for
us pretty well over six years.

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.350
Rewrote it once, and again
evaluated the options,

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:40.350
and there were no real good
solutions that were good enough

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.210
to convince us to migrate.

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:47.890
But early last year, we started
hitting a couple of problems.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:50.880
So one of the problems that
we've had over the years

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:52.435
has there's been no
service available

00:27:52.435 --> 00:27:54.060
for a challenge or
a problem that we've

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:55.420
been hitting at scale.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:57.180
And so we've had to
build a lot of custom

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:58.830
implementations of things.

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:03.480
And so we were spending a lot
of time maintaining that system,

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.180
not only on the
infrastructure, so it

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:08.220
was running on
bare metal servers,

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.930
it was running at softlayer.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:13.320
And not on the infrastructure
itself in terms of the servers,

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:15.360
but also all of the
custom code that we

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:19.410
had to write to do things like
pulling data and displaying

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:24.090
graphs and calculating maths
functions on the data, sums,

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:25.470
averages, that kind of stuff.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.255
We had to build
it all ourselves.

00:28:27.255 --> 00:28:28.830
And that's not a
good use of our time

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.980
when we're a relatively small
company of 20 people now,

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:34.970
but it's grown
gradually, organically

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:37.422
as the company has scaled.

00:28:37.422 --> 00:28:38.880
And it's not a good
use of our time

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:40.470
to be building
what is essentially

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:43.170
undifferentiated heavy lifting.

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:45.700
We also had problems
with scaling it.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:49.470
MongoDB does scale,
contrary to popular belief.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:53.130
But you have to put a lot of
time into managing the shards

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:55.030
and managing the replication.

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:58.410
And in particular, when
you add a new replica set,

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:01.020
you've got to migrate that
data over to it, which does

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:03.240
have an impact.

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.640
And then keeping all
of this data in memory,

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:07.920
so that when you're
loading your dashboard,

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:10.680
the graphs load
within the second.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.630
And the typical use
case is loading data

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:15.990
from the last day or so.

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:19.830
And so we have to keep that
data in memory to make it fast.

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:22.860
And then slightly older
data goes onto SSDs,

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:24.675
because hard disks
are just too slow.

00:29:24.675 --> 00:29:26.790
And this is actually
really expensive.

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:32.040
Even with modern pricing,
having really high spec service

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:32.820
is not cheap.

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:36.090
And we had many terabytes
of RAM and many hundreds

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:40.870
of terabytes of SSDs
just to store this data.

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:41.870
So we did an evaluation.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:43.400
We looked at different options.

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:45.150
And there are a couple
of different things

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:46.260
that we could have used.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:50.400
But we ultimately selected
OpenTSDB and Bigtable

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.535
as the storage engine
for a couple of reasons.

00:29:53.535 --> 00:29:55.410
So the first of these
is that it is a managed

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:58.650
service with a small team.

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:02.130
And we don't want to be spending
time managing the underlying

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:03.400
infrastructure.

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:05.430
So we don't want to
be upgrading RAM,

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.980
we don't want to have to put
in tickets to get RAM upgraded,

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:10.396
we don't have to deal with
disk failures, all of the stuff

00:30:10.396 --> 00:30:13.875
that it's just not a
good use of our time.

00:30:13.875 --> 00:30:15.810
Bigtable is a completely
managed service,

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:19.420
and so this is one of
the selling points.

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:19.920
I

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.470
Now OpenTSDB is an
actively developed project,

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:23.544
it's open source.

00:30:23.544 --> 00:30:24.960
And there's a lot
of functionality

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:26.910
built into the product,
that meant that we

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:28.170
wouldn't have to build it.

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:30.128
And there's a few things
that we've contributed

00:30:30.128 --> 00:30:31.920
upstream, a couple of fixes.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:34.860
But for the most part, it's
feature-complete from what

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:36.930
we need.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:38.640
We also get linear scalability.

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:42.240
And this is a really
important point for planning,

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:44.700
because we can
just add new nodes,

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:49.370
and we know we're going to
get an additional 10,000 QPS.

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:52.680
So this makes it really easy
to understand how much more

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:55.600
capacity we need and
when, and exactly how much

00:30:55.600 --> 00:31:00.350
it's going to cost, because
each node is a fixed cost.

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:02.976
And it's a specialist
data store.

00:31:02.976 --> 00:31:07.290
Bigtable is specifically
designed for time series data.

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:09.510
And so all of these
combined means

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:12.050
it can be significantly
more efficient

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:16.390
than moving MongoDB over to
Compute Engine, for example,

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:20.060
or using a generic SQL database.

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:22.400
The whole point of
Bigtable is to store

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:23.770
this exact kind of data.

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:27.980
So the migration.

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:30.539
Now, as a monitoring
product, it needs to be up,

00:31:30.539 --> 00:31:31.580
it needs to be available.

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:33.707
And we run on the principle
that your monitoring

00:31:33.707 --> 00:31:36.290
should be more reliable than the
infrastructure it is actually

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:37.370
monitoring.

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:39.680
So this meant we couldn't
have a period of downtime

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:42.410
whilst we moved the data over.

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:46.820
Also, we're migrating across
two different environments,

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:50.030
between softlayer
over to Google.

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.620
And we looked at a couple
of different options,

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:54.710
including VPNs and
DirectConnect, which

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:57.830
are good options, but ultimately
settled on just sending it

00:31:57.830 --> 00:31:59.020
over the internet.

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:00.950
And this is because all
the internal systems

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:03.050
within [INAUDIBLE]
our micro services,

00:32:03.050 --> 00:32:06.560
so we could just extract it,
put it into Google Cloud,

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:09.900
and expose it over
HTTPS, with of course,

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.710
the appropriate
whitelisting for our IPs,

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.120
an have the data going over the
internet with minimal latency.

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:18.620
We were able to pick a zone
very close to the softlayer data

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:19.120
center.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:23.700
With two systems
running, we needed

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.400
to write the data from
the production environment

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:28.290
on softlayer into Google.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:31.650
And this has to happen
at the exact same time.

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:35.160
We picked a date, we
went live with Bigtable,

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:37.440
but behind the scenes,
so customers were still

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:38.830
using the old system.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:41.070
But data was being
written into both.

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.010
And from that point,
all the historical data,

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:45.630
so going backwards,
could then be

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:48.830
migrated in our own timeframe.

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:53.310
And so we picked the historical
data, sent it over to Bigtable,

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:55.680
and then flipped a
feature flag, which

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:57.860
meant the reads went over.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:00.300
So the writes were always
going, but then the reads

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.382
were going on a
per-customer basis.

00:33:02.382 --> 00:33:05.100
So as we completed
each migration,

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:07.770
we flipped that flag,
and customers were then

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:11.060
having their reads
go over to Bigtable.

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:14.320
And despite this connectivity
over the internet,

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:16.590
we gotten significantly
better performance.

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:18.930
And the graphs,
whatever time range

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:22.500
you might pick, from 30
minutes to 30 months,

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.580
we get pretty much
exact same loading time

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:31.150
despite the latency of
going over the internet.

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:33.389
And of course, we're
monitoring company.

00:33:33.389 --> 00:33:34.930
So we had detailed
metrics so that we

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:38.386
could compare the
performance before and after.

00:33:38.386 --> 00:33:41.080
And this allowed us to
verify that the migration

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:44.020
from our testing
over into production

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:48.910
was working as expected, but
also allowed us to pick out

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.710
an interesting
design flaw, which

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:53.430
I'll show you in a moment.

00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:55.880
So this is the new system.

00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:58.100
We also took some time
about two years ago

00:33:58.100 --> 00:34:01.671
to get rid of that MongoDB
queuing system, get rid of PHP,

00:34:01.671 --> 00:34:03.170
and replace it with
a proper system.

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:06.440
So that's Kafka and Storm.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:09.110
Today we might look at
Google Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:12.080
but it wasn't available at the
time we started that project.

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:15.110
Kafka is a great
queuing system, and we

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:17.810
were able to just port
in our Python code.

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:20.480
And we routed it from PHP
into Python into Storm,

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:24.500
because it has a wrapper, so
it doesn't have to be in Java.

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:27.409
The flow is then the same, so it
goes through the rules engine,

00:34:27.409 --> 00:34:30.830
which was also rewritten into
Python, so it's done properly.

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:34.100
And notifications,
very similar as well.

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:36.230
And that remains on
the softlayer slide

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:37.448
and it's still there today.

00:34:37.448 --> 00:34:38.989
Over the next couple
of months, we're

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:40.929
moving everything
over to Google Cloud,

00:34:40.929 --> 00:34:43.940
but the Bigtable project
was the first one

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:45.770
that allowed us to
move over this really

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:47.960
core part of the product.

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:49.760
And then on the
Google Cloud side

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:52.790
of things, because
it's a micro service,

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:54.920
the API itself for
querying the graphs.

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:56.690
So if you load up
our interface and you

00:34:56.690 --> 00:34:58.840
inspect the code in
the console, you'll

00:34:58.840 --> 00:35:02.360
be able to see the internal API
requests that are happening.

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:05.750
And this hits the
Google load balancer,

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:08.870
then connects into OpenTSDB.

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:10.550
And this runs on
Container Engine

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:13.450
because it is itself
stateless, and allows

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:15.260
us to scale it as we need to.

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:17.390
It runs on containers.

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:20.150
And that has an
official connector,

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:24.950
which allows OpenTSDB to use of
Bigtable as a storage engine.

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:27.350
And then we deploy this
across multiple zones,

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:32.000
And so that we get
the redundancy.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:34.930
Now, two interesting
lessons that we'd learnt.

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:39.260
Now during our testing
and the development phase,

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:43.160
we spun up the Bigtable
cluster and started

00:35:43.160 --> 00:35:44.780
sending in some
writes so that we

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:46.640
could test to see what
the performance was

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:48.200
and how it would work.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:50.680
And we were very
quickly dismayed to see

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:52.430
that our estimations
were blown completely

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:55.430
out of the water in terms
of the number of requests

00:35:55.430 --> 00:35:59.750
that we would need, and more
or less every single metric

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:04.070
that we were writing was calling
a single API calls on Bigtable.

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:07.350
This is just a subset,
it's not our full workload.

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:11.360
So we were seeing very quickly,
60,000 requests per second

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.500
into Bigtable.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.130
And because you pay
on a per note basis,

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:18.050
and each node gives
you 10,000 requests,

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:20.960
our estimations of what we
would actually going to do

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:22.370
were completely incorrect.

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:25.560
After talking with the
Google support team,

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:28.400
we discovered the OpenTSDB
ship with an outdated version

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:29.510
of the driver.

00:36:29.510 --> 00:36:33.400
And we were able to update to
a development version, which

00:36:33.400 --> 00:36:35.360
significantly reduced
the number of API

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:38.690
calls because it had introduced
a new feature that allows

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:40.370
you to batch those calls.

00:36:40.370 --> 00:36:43.340
So we're still writing
the same amount of data,

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:47.210
but we were able to go from
60,000 API calls per second

00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:48.240
down to three.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:52.610
And that allowed us to half
the node size and half the cost

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:55.880
of this test, and meant that
our estimations were indeed

00:36:55.880 --> 00:36:58.550
correct, and we could
continue with the project.

00:36:58.550 --> 00:37:01.400
So the lesson learn here
is, stay with the latest

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:05.000
version of the driver and also
test with development versions,

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:08.690
because the official supported
latest version might actually

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:11.240
be out of date, and Google's
technical support are

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:15.790
really good at helping diagnose
these kinds of problems.

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:18.490
The second one is a
little more complicated.

00:37:18.490 --> 00:37:23.110
We found this after we'd
completed the migration,

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:25.490
because we'd tested
with small accounts.

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:27.610
And we have some customers
who are monitoring

00:37:27.610 --> 00:37:29.350
huge numbers of servers.

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:32.064
And we've found that the
row keys that you set,

00:37:32.064 --> 00:37:33.730
it's really important
to ensure that you

00:37:33.730 --> 00:37:37.090
have the correct cardinality
when scanning rows.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:38.680
And the first
approach that we took

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:41.310
resulted in too many scans.

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:44.380
And every account has a
unique ID and metric name

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.200
like load average or disk usage.

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:49.780
And that was the
original key design.

00:37:49.780 --> 00:37:51.610
But by querying
on that, it meant

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:55.560
that we had to not only
scan, but filter to map

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:58.120
that metric for all devices.

00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:02.910
So a query on a particular
device for say, system load,

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:09.820
had to scan and filter every
single device on the account.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.880
And this meant that
the number of scan rows

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:17.100
was the number of devices
multiplied by the time range.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:21.160
So for big accounts, all
the devices for that account

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:23.290
would get queried and scanned.

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:26.620
And this reduced the
performance of the system

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:28.420
as the customer got bigger.

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:30.280
And we discovered this
with our monitoring

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:34.790
once we started to get to
production to test that.

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:39.890
So we refactored the key so
the only very specific devices

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:41.100
are queried.

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:44.540
So we went from the account
ID and the metric name

00:38:44.540 --> 00:38:46.340
to include the item ID.

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:49.370
So an item is a server or a URL.

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:52.460
And we included that
in the key itself.

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:55.220
Now, tagged
filtering in OpenTSDB

00:38:55.220 --> 00:38:57.837
can still reduce
performance because it

00:38:57.837 --> 00:38:59.420
increases the amount
of filtering that

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:00.128
needs to be done.

00:39:00.128 --> 00:39:02.060
So you have to be
careful with that.

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:04.970
But it meant that
instead of going

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:10.430
from querying every single
row, the scanner only

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:14.774
has to do it based
on the time range.

00:39:14.774 --> 00:39:17.315
So this is what it looks like
with the refactored key design.

00:39:17.315 --> 00:39:20.180
And if we compare it to
the original key design,

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:21.650
you can see the
amount of data that

00:39:21.650 --> 00:39:24.350
has to be scanned is
significantly reduced.

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:30.960
So in the end, we were
able to migrate everything

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:32.860
over-- we've just
completed the migration--

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:36.220
and have a linearly
scalable system, which

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:39.460
has high availability
across multiple zones;

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:41.777
allows us to build
new product features,

00:39:41.777 --> 00:39:43.360
because we don't
have to deal with any

00:39:43.360 --> 00:39:46.780
of the operational overhead of
running servers and building

00:39:46.780 --> 00:39:48.940
features that just
don't differentiate

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:51.370
on the standard level.

00:39:51.370 --> 00:39:53.470
And we get that
with lower costs.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.840
And we're able to reduce
our cost of running

00:39:55.840 --> 00:40:00.790
bare metal along with MongoDB by
three times by migrating over.

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:05.660
Now the original code
was written by me.

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:08.120
And I had real
engineers to rewrite it.

00:40:08.120 --> 00:40:11.410
And these are the guys on
the team who did that work,

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:14.660
and worked with our operations
team and our engineers,

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:17.150
and also the Bigtable
engineering team,

00:40:17.150 --> 00:40:18.770
and of course, OpenTSDB.

00:40:18.770 --> 00:40:22.100
So I wanted to say
thanks to those people,

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:24.352
and just show exactly
what this powers.

00:40:24.352 --> 00:40:27.170
It powers our Apple TV
app, powers our mobile app,

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:32.220
and of course, the graphing
within the product itself.

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.032
All right, so I'm going to hand
you over to Anthony Fox now.

00:40:35.032 --> 00:40:35.740
Thanks very much.

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:38.946
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:38.946 --> 00:40:41.430
ANTHONY FOX: Thank you.

00:40:41.430 --> 00:40:42.530
Thank you.

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:44.480
So my name's Anthony Fox.

00:40:44.480 --> 00:40:48.230
I'm from Virginia, where I work
for a small analytics-focused

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:49.880
company called CCRI.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:53.390
And I'm going to be
talking today about GeoMesa

00:40:53.390 --> 00:40:59.750
and how you can use open source
to manage spatiotemporal data

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:03.770
on top of Bigtable, and also on
top of some other Google Cloud

00:41:03.770 --> 00:41:06.410
architectural components.

00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:11.070
So just to level set,
traditional GIS spatial data

00:41:11.070 --> 00:41:13.530
is things like points,
lines, and polygons--

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:18.720
so natural features, geographic
features, political boundaries,

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:21.560
those things that are fairly
static and not very big,

00:41:21.560 --> 00:41:24.260
not necessarily the best
use case on top of Bigtable

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:25.920
that you could use that.

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:29.420
There's also a huge amount
of satellite imagery

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:31.640
that is being produced
every single day as part

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:32.390
of the keynote.

00:41:32.390 --> 00:41:36.050
We heard that Planet Labs
was imaging the entire Earth

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:37.730
every three or four
hours, I think,

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:40.462
which is producing an
enormous amount of data.

00:41:40.462 --> 00:41:42.170
But then there's also
the data that we're

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:45.470
going to focus on today,
which is the kind of data

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:49.040
that companies like
exactEarth collect.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:54.240
And this is a growing area of
data, of spatiotemporal data.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:57.620
So this is a
visualization of the data

00:41:57.620 --> 00:42:00.110
that exactEarth collects
from their constellation

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:01.130
of satellites.

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:02.205
This is AIS data.

00:42:02.205 --> 00:42:06.680
AIS stands for the automatic
identification system.

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:10.100
It's a protocol that
all marine vessels

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:13.500
broadcast their location
on every six seconds.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.890
So every vessel in the world
broadcasts their location

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:17.330
every six seconds.

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:23.120
And exactEarth's
satellites pick that up

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:26.270
as they spin around the
globe and downlink that

00:42:26.270 --> 00:42:29.330
to navigation systems,
safety systems,

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:33.094
routing systems, all
kinds of other systems.

00:42:33.094 --> 00:42:34.760
So there's the satellite
collected data,

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:37.630
but then there's data collected
from connected devices.

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:41.420
So the data that an
organization like Strava

00:42:41.420 --> 00:42:46.160
collects, so Strava is a social
media application for fitness

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:48.260
activities.

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:52.590
So exactEarth collects maritime
data every six seconds.

00:42:52.590 --> 00:42:56.930
It produces, I think right now
about 8 million records a day.

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:00.187
And they're launching a
whole suite of new satellites

00:43:00.187 --> 00:43:02.270
where they're going to
collect orders of magnitude

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:03.230
more than that.

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:07.430
Strava collects every
second, a measurement

00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:09.980
is taken for each activity.

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:14.570
And that measurement has things
like heart rate, elevation,

00:43:14.570 --> 00:43:16.880
time, location.

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:19.250
So they produce a lot
of data per activity,

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:21.212
and then across all
of the activities.

00:43:21.212 --> 00:43:22.670
These are some
density maps that we

00:43:22.670 --> 00:43:26.000
produced from ingesting
Strava data into GeoMesa.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:29.600
This is commutes in downtown
San Francisco, which

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:32.851
pretty much covers all of
the roads in downtown San

00:43:32.851 --> 00:43:33.350
Francisco.

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:37.160
So spatiotemporal
data is growing.

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:40.250
Volumes of it is growing,
it's very high velocity.

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:43.580
In order to manage it, I'm
one of the original founders

00:43:43.580 --> 00:43:45.950
of the open source
GeoMesa Project,

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:51.050
which is an Eclipse Foundation
location tech working

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:53.330
group open source project.

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:58.910
It's a toolkit for managing and
analyzing spatiotemporal data,

00:43:58.910 --> 00:44:01.580
both at rest and in stream.

00:44:01.580 --> 00:44:04.850
For managing and analyzing
the data at rest,

00:44:04.850 --> 00:44:08.360
it has integrations with
Cassandra, HBase, Accumulo,

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:12.950
and the original NoSQL
database, Bigtable,

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:15.650
which we're going to
talk in detail about.

00:44:15.650 --> 00:44:21.260
For streaming data, it has
integration with Apache Kafka.

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:25.160
It's actually the same API that
it exposes on top of Bigtable,

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:28.500
it exposes on top of Kafka,
which is kind of interesting.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.610
And for analyzing the data,
it has fairly deep integration

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:36.950
with Spark SQL for doing
spatiotemporal operations.

00:44:36.950 --> 00:44:39.590
OK, so now we're
going to deep dive

00:44:39.590 --> 00:44:43.520
into how you might index
data, spatiotemporal data

00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:44.270
in Bigtable.

00:44:44.270 --> 00:44:46.950
So remember, Bigtable
has one index.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:51.740
It's a lexicographically
sorted key.

00:44:51.740 --> 00:44:55.100
So as an example of
what you shouldn't do,

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:59.150
consider if you concatenated
latitude and longitude.

00:44:59.150 --> 00:45:01.550
So your row key becomes
what you see there.

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:06.170
This is the Moscone Center's
latitude and longitude,

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:12.299
37.7839, negative 122.4012.

00:45:12.299 --> 00:45:14.090
The typical query that
you're going to make

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:15.710
is going to bound
those dimensions.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:18.440
So you're going to look for
things around the Moscone

00:45:18.440 --> 00:45:19.400
Center.

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:22.700
And if you do that and
you scan Bigtable's

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.490
lexicographically
sorted index, you're

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:31.100
going to get data points that
are near Fukushima, Japan,

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:34.400
because they happen to
share the same latitude,

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:38.600
and they sort lexicographically
near each other.

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:42.100
So data that is very far
apart in physical space

00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:45.094
is close in Bigtable index
space, and that's not good.

00:45:45.094 --> 00:45:46.260
So we don't want to do that.

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:48.890
So we have to be a little
bit more clever than that.

00:45:48.890 --> 00:45:53.082
And so we use something
called space-filling curves.

00:45:53.082 --> 00:45:55.540
So there's lots of different
kinds of space-filling curves.

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:57.370
The whole point of a
space-filling curve

00:45:57.370 --> 00:46:02.030
is to encode multiple dimensions
into a single dimension

00:46:02.030 --> 00:46:05.800
so that nearness is maintained.

00:46:05.800 --> 00:46:08.500
So things that are near
each other in physical space

00:46:08.500 --> 00:46:11.619
are near each other
in index space.

00:46:11.619 --> 00:46:12.910
So here's a couple of examples.

00:46:12.910 --> 00:46:16.660
On the far right you
have the Hilbert curve,

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:20.395
and you have multiple
iterations of the Hilbert curve.

00:46:20.395 --> 00:46:22.720
The Hilbert curve
has great locality.

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:24.440
On the left you've
got the Z-order curve,

00:46:24.440 --> 00:46:25.450
which is incredibly simple.

00:46:25.450 --> 00:46:27.241
And so we're going to
walk through a deeper

00:46:27.241 --> 00:46:28.372
dive of the Z-order curve.

00:46:28.372 --> 00:46:29.830
There's also an
interesting library

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:32.500
that Google has put
out called S2, which

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:37.060
treats the spherical
earth as a cube,

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:39.220
and then overlays a
space-filling curve

00:46:39.220 --> 00:46:41.740
on each one of those
faces, and in doing

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:47.740
so minimizes the distortion that
is typical of map projections.

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:50.110
All right, so let's walk
through the simplest

00:46:50.110 --> 00:46:51.760
space-filling curve
and how it gets

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:57.040
used so you get some intuition
for how you produce these keys.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:01.060
So we're going to encode the
coordinates of the Moscone

00:47:01.060 --> 00:47:06.140
Center into a 32-bit
integer Z index.

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:07.840
So we have 32 bits.

00:47:07.840 --> 00:47:10.780
We have two dimensions,
so 16 bits per dimension.

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:13.567
We're going to
scale the latitude

00:47:13.567 --> 00:47:14.650
and longitude coordinates.

00:47:14.650 --> 00:47:15.774
They're bounded dimensions.

00:47:15.774 --> 00:47:19.110
So for instance, longitude is
bounded negative 180 to 180.

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:25.030
We're going to map negative 180
to 0, and 180 to 2 to the 16th.

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:28.510
We'll do the same
scaling for the latitude.

00:47:28.510 --> 00:47:31.000
And we have integer
representations

00:47:31.000 --> 00:47:34.370
of our x and y-coordinates.

00:47:34.370 --> 00:47:36.800
Then we take the
binary representation.

00:47:36.800 --> 00:47:39.480
So there's 16-bit
strings, essentially.

00:47:39.480 --> 00:47:43.240
Interleave those, convert
back to an integer,

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:45.600
and that's our Bigtable key.

00:47:45.600 --> 00:47:47.380
And so what we've
effectively done

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:49.630
is we've linearized this
multi-dimensional space.

00:47:49.630 --> 00:47:52.360
So in this chart
on the right, you

00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:56.050
can see a kind of conceptual
notion of a Bigtable index

00:47:56.050 --> 00:48:00.460
going from 0 to 2 to
the 32, and the point

00:48:00.460 --> 00:48:06.200
on that index where the Moscone
Center lands in index space.

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:08.380
What's really nice about
this is that things

00:48:08.380 --> 00:48:11.500
that are near each other sort
near each other in Bigtable.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:15.670
And region queries, which
is your 95% use case

00:48:15.670 --> 00:48:18.850
spatiotemporal query,
turns into a range scan.

00:48:18.850 --> 00:48:21.730
So the range that you're
looking at there for that box

00:48:21.730 --> 00:48:27.040
is just a scan from a starting
integer to an ending integer.

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:31.510
And we get the magic of Bigtable
handling of partitioning

00:48:31.510 --> 00:48:35.020
and load balancing
for free, because we

00:48:35.020 --> 00:48:39.610
have this nice machinery
behind this structure

00:48:39.610 --> 00:48:42.070
that we've set up.

00:48:42.070 --> 00:48:44.530
So if we jump back, there are
some additional properties

00:48:44.530 --> 00:48:47.170
to wrap up on
space-filling curve.

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:48.760
They have this
recursive structure.

00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:51.460
So if you look at
the Z order curve,

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:56.290
the first picture
is two bits, right?

00:48:56.290 --> 00:49:00.820
So it has this
quadtree decomposition.

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:02.830
And you start adding
more bits, but you

00:49:02.830 --> 00:49:04.600
can do these common
prefix scans,

00:49:04.600 --> 00:49:07.480
which is one of the things
that Bigtable is best at.

00:49:07.480 --> 00:49:13.420
And those prefix scans map
to regions on the earth.

00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:15.730
They naturally extend to
higher order dimensions

00:49:15.730 --> 00:49:19.060
with just a few tricks here and
there so you can encode time

00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:23.380
into the Z order curve, and
you get a Z3 curve or three

00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:25.240
dimensional Hilbert curve.

00:49:25.240 --> 00:49:27.820
And there are some
extensions to Z order curves

00:49:27.820 --> 00:49:32.320
that allow you to index
nonpoint geometry,

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:35.230
so geometries with spatial
extent like line strings

00:49:35.230 --> 00:49:37.090
and polygons.

00:49:37.090 --> 00:49:40.524
So that covers the
basics of how you

00:49:40.524 --> 00:49:42.190
use a space-filling
curve in conjunction

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:44.579
with Bigtable's model.

00:49:44.579 --> 00:49:46.870
So another thing that I'd
like to talk about, something

00:49:46.870 --> 00:49:49.720
that we've spent a lot of
time in GeoMesa, but I think

00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:54.340
is a natural evolution
of a developer

00:49:54.340 --> 00:49:57.040
working with Bigtable.

00:49:57.040 --> 00:49:59.650
In Bigtable, you
have this one index.

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:01.840
So you design your
table structure

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:05.200
so that it maps to the query
patterns that you expect.

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:07.930
And you make those query
patterns really, really fast.

00:50:07.930 --> 00:50:09.850
That's really good, you
feel good about that.

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:11.590
And then suddenly
your users start

00:50:11.590 --> 00:50:13.300
doing different query patterns.

00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:16.090
And you realize that
your index structure

00:50:16.090 --> 00:50:19.600
is good for some queries, but
terrible for other queries.

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:24.760
So much as you'd add an index
in a relational database,

00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:28.930
in Bigtable, adding
an index amounts

00:50:28.930 --> 00:50:32.770
to redundantly storing
your data in another table

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:36.010
with a different
index structure.

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:37.600
So in this query
example then I'm

00:50:37.600 --> 00:50:41.210
showing here from
a Strava table,

00:50:41.210 --> 00:50:45.360
there is a spatial
predicate the ST contains,

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:47.930
an athlete ID predicate.

00:50:47.930 --> 00:50:50.330
You don't want to scan
all of the data that

00:50:50.330 --> 00:50:53.690
matches that spatial predicate
and filter down to the athlete

00:50:53.690 --> 00:50:57.500
ID, you want an index on athlete
IDs, so those are really fast.

00:50:57.500 --> 00:51:01.850
So in GeoMesa, we
have a query planner

00:51:01.850 --> 00:51:06.500
that will inspect the query
and find the predicate that

00:51:06.500 --> 00:51:09.180
has the least cost
in query execution,

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:13.820
and dispatch the
query to that index--

00:51:13.820 --> 00:51:17.780
in this case, the Strava
activities index--

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:20.490
and then apply spatial
predicate filtering

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.207
in a post-processing phase.

00:51:22.207 --> 00:51:23.290
This is a typical pattern.

00:51:23.290 --> 00:51:25.706
So either you use this through
a library or it's something

00:51:25.706 --> 00:51:30.370
that you can adopt as you
begin to engage with Bigtable.

00:51:30.370 --> 00:51:31.850
So we've covered
spatial indexing,

00:51:31.850 --> 00:51:34.160
we've covered redundantly
storing your data

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:36.280
with multiple index structures.

00:51:36.280 --> 00:51:38.180
What about analytical workloads?

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:42.087
So we heard that there's no
query time joins in Bigtable,

00:51:42.087 --> 00:51:43.670
but you still want
to answer questions

00:51:43.670 --> 00:51:47.000
like in the exactEarth use case,
what is the optimal time of day

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:48.710
to traverse the Panama Canal.

00:51:48.710 --> 00:51:51.350
It takes, I think on the
order of seven to eight hours

00:51:51.350 --> 00:51:53.930
to traverse the Panama Canal.

00:51:53.930 --> 00:51:57.535
So picking the right time
can be a big cost savings.

00:51:57.535 --> 00:51:59.420
So that's the kind
of analytical query

00:51:59.420 --> 00:52:03.020
that you might ask
of exactEarth's data.

00:52:03.020 --> 00:52:05.600
An analytical query you
might ask of Strava's data

00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:09.230
is, what are the typical
commuter routes of workplaces

00:52:09.230 --> 00:52:11.210
in downtown San Francisco?

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:14.300
These are joins and
aggregations that Bigtable just

00:52:14.300 --> 00:52:17.390
doesn't provide at query time.

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:21.500
But Bigtable does support
loading your data out

00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:24.440
of Bigtable and into
something like Spark.

00:52:24.440 --> 00:52:27.380
So you can use this
Spark SQL in conjunction

00:52:27.380 --> 00:52:32.360
with Google Cloud Dataproc
to recover the ability

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:38.000
to do joins in aggregations and
execute analytical workloads

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:40.420
on top of your Bigtable data.

00:52:40.420 --> 00:52:45.030
And GeoMesa adds a bunch
of extensions to Spark SQL,

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:48.110
including geometry types,
spatial predicates,

00:52:48.110 --> 00:52:51.090
and geometry
processing functions.

00:52:51.090 --> 00:52:55.310
So let's dissect
an analytical query

00:52:55.310 --> 00:53:02.050
and see how the whole suite
of Google Cloud products

00:53:02.050 --> 00:53:04.520
allows you to execute
this particular workload.

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:06.730
So this is the
Panama Canal query.

00:53:06.730 --> 00:53:10.990
We've got aggregations,
averaging counts, group bys.

00:53:10.990 --> 00:53:12.490
We've got geospatial predicates.

00:53:12.490 --> 00:53:14.200
In this case, it's ST contains.

00:53:14.200 --> 00:53:15.820
We want to filter
it down to things

00:53:15.820 --> 00:53:17.890
that are around Panama Canal.

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:19.880
We've got geometry types.

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:24.810
We're creating a polygon
predicate on a one hour

00:53:24.810 --> 00:53:27.700
geometry column within Bigtable.

00:53:27.700 --> 00:53:32.380
So what happens is, you spin up
a Dataproc cluster of whatever

00:53:32.380 --> 00:53:35.320
size elastically
scaled to the workload

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:38.110
that you expect for that
particular use case.

00:53:38.110 --> 00:53:43.300
You submit your Spark SQL
query and GeoMesa's extensions

00:53:43.300 --> 00:53:47.590
intercept the query, discover
the spatial predicate,

00:53:47.590 --> 00:53:51.490
and use the spatial predicate
to compute the ranges out

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:55.180
of Bigtable that they'll
use to provision the RDD.

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:57.670
So only the data that
matches that predicate

00:53:57.670 --> 00:54:01.270
will come into the Spark
in memory run time.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:05.260
And at that point, Spark then
spins and does its aggregations

00:54:05.260 --> 00:54:08.060
and GroupBy operations.

00:54:08.060 --> 00:54:11.140
So we've been able to recover
this analytical workload

00:54:11.140 --> 00:54:13.840
capability by leveraging
additional components

00:54:13.840 --> 00:54:16.450
of the Google suite.

00:54:16.450 --> 00:54:20.350
And you'll discover that you
should time your traversal

00:54:20.350 --> 00:54:24.760
of the Panama Canal
to about 0600 GMT,

00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:30.530
which is the least
trafficked hour of the day.

00:54:30.530 --> 00:54:34.750
So that covers what I was
going to talk about today.

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:38.080
As a note, this particular
slide is out of date.

00:54:38.080 --> 00:54:39.700
It says 131 snapshot.

00:54:39.700 --> 00:54:44.020
But the release was cut, 131
was cut maybe an hour ago.

00:54:44.020 --> 00:54:47.000
So it's just in time.

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:49.870
are my coordinates
for contacting me.

00:54:49.870 --> 00:54:52.200
This is available now.

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:53.740
Yeah, thank you.

00:54:53.740 --> 00:54:55.840
